Why this fork exists
--------------------

XML 1.0 Fifth Edition redefined the NameStartChar [4] and NameChar [4a]
productions. The First Edition enumerated the permitted characters as
Letter | Digit | Extender | CombiningChar lists derived from Unicode 2.0
categories; the Fifth Edition replaced that with broad codepoint ranges and a
short list of exclusions. The change was made precisely so that names would
not have to be re-ratified against each new Unicode version.

encoding/xml still ships the First Edition tables. Its own comment says so:

    // These tables were generated by cut and paste from Appendix B of
    // the XML spec at https://www.xml.com/axml/testaxml.htm

Appendix B is the First Edition's enumeration. The consequence is that
encoding/xml rejects 936,990 codepoints in element and attribute names that a
conformant XML 1.0 5e parser must accept — measured by walking the whole
Unicode scalar range and comparing the two productions; the difference is
purely additive, no codepoint that encoding/xml accepts is lost.

This mattered here for two reasons. First, real conformance cases fail on it:
the XSD test suite's saxonData/XmlVersions groups xv001 and xv004 are titled
"Use newly-allowed name characters ..." and both parse "<Dĳkstra>". Second,
and worse, the library contradicted itself: xslt validates a *computed* name
against 5e at a dozen call sites, so a stylesheet could construct an element
name that this library's own parser would then refuse to read back.

Post-correcting the stdlib decoder from outside is not possible. On an invalid
name Decoder.name sets d.err and returns no token, so Token returns (nil, err)
— there is nothing to correct — and d.err is latched, so the remainder of the
document is unreachable. A pre-pass over the bytes is circular: deciding which
bytes are names requires the parse being delegated.


The first patch: XML 1.0 5e names
---------------------------------

Four substantive lines, in the two name-checking functions. Both isName and
isNameString have the same shape, and each carries two of the four:

    -	if !unicode.Is(first, c) {
    +	if !xmlname.IsNameStartRune(c) { // go-xml: XML 1.0 5e [4] NameStartChar

    -		if !unicode.Is(first, c) && !unicode.Is(second, c) {
    +		if !xmlname.IsNameRune(c) { // go-xml: XML 1.0 5e [4a] NameChar

Plus one import of internal/xmlname. No Unicode data is written here: xmlname
holds the single 5e transcription that xdm also exports to the sibling
parsers, so the tokeniser and the schema languages cannot drift apart.

Two things are deleted rather than changed, neither of them behaviour:

  * the `first` and `second` RangeTables, ~330 lines, now dead;
  * everything from EscapeText to the end of the file except procInst — the
    printer, the escaping tables and emitCDATA belong to marshal.go, which is
    not forked. This package is used as a tokeniser only: xdm consumes
    Decoder, Token and the token types, and nothing else.

The second patch: XML 1.1
-------------------------

Upstream refuses any version but 1.0 outright, so an XML 1.1 document never
parses. xdm used to work around that by rewriting the declaration to 1.0
before the tokeniser saw it, which is worse than refusing: the document was
admitted and then read under the wrong language's rules.

The version is now read from the declaration and kept, in Decoder.version11,
and it is set from the XML declaration and nowhere else. That is deliberate:
the version is a property of the document text, so no caller-supplied option
can assert a version the text contradicts. The default is 1.0 — an absent
declaration, or one naming 1.0, gets none of 1.1's relaxations.

Four changes carry it, all marked "go-xml: XML 1.1" in the source:

  * Decoder gains `version11` and `sawDecl`. sawDecl stops a stray <?xml?>
    later in the document from retroactively changing the version of text
    already read.

  * The version gate in the <?xml?> branch of rawToken accepts "1.1" as well
    as "1.0", and records which. Anything else is still refused, with the
    error text widened to name both supported versions.

  * isInCharacterRange11 [2] and isRestrictedChar [2a] join upstream's
    isInCharacterRange, with charOK/literalCharOK selecting per version.

  * d.text validates a numeric character reference AT THE POINT OF EXPANSION
    and records the byte span it produced, in a new `refSpan`. The trailing
    character scan then skips those spans. This is the structural change:
    XML 1.1 §2.2 admits a RestrictedChar only as a character reference, and
    upstream's single trailing scan runs after expansion, where a literal
    control character and a referenced one are the same bytes. The two have
    to be told apart, and only the expansion site knows which is which.

    A side effect is that references are now checked BEFORE \r normalisation
    rather than after, which is also where upstream should have checked them.

  * d.text normalises NEL (#x85) and #x2028 to #xA under 1.1 only, per §2.11,
    including \r#x85 as a single two-character line end. The lead bytes are
    already in the buffer when the sequence completes, so they are unwritten.

Re-applying this on a newer Go means re-doing the d.text work by hand; it is
the only part that is not a local substitution. Everything else is additive.

Nothing else is changed. In particular entity handling, namespace translation
and error text are upstream's, and XML 1.0 behaviour is unchanged except that
a character reference to a non-character is now caught at the reference
rather than after normalisation.


Re-forking from a newer Go
--------------------------

    cp $GOROOT/src/encoding/xml/xml.go internal/xmlfork/xml.go

then: rename the package to xmlfork; re-apply BOTH patches above; delete the
`first`/`second` tables and the post-EscapeText tail as described.

Every semantic change is marked in the source with a "go-xml:" comment, so
`grep -n "go-xml:" xml.go` enumerates the whole patch. The first patch is four
local substitutions. The second is mostly additive — new functions and two
Decoder fields — except for the d.text changes, which interleave with upstream
code and have to be re-applied by reading rather than by patch.

Verify with the upstream test suite, which is the real check that the fork is
still a correct tokeniser. It cannot run against this trimmed copy — it
exercises Marshal and Unmarshal, which are not here — so run it against a
patched copy of the *full* stdlib package instead:

    mkdir -p /tmp/upstreamcheck/xml
    cp $GOROOT/src/encoding/xml/*.go /tmp/upstreamcheck/xml/
    # Apply the same four lines to /tmp/upstreamcheck/xml/xml.go, but spell
    # them xdm.IsNameStartChar / xdm.IsNameChar: that check lives outside this
    # module, and internal/ is not importable from outside. Those two are
    # thin wrappers over the xmlname predicates the fork calls, so the code
    # under test is the same.
    # Add a go.mod requiring github.com/knroy/go-xml, replaced to this tree.
    cd /tmp/upstreamcheck && go test ./xml/ -count=1

At Go 1.26.4 that is 84 tests, all passing.

The Go version is therefore a conformance dependency twice over: once for the
behaviour of the tokeniser this fork tracks, and once for the test suite that
certifies the patch. See docs/testing.md.
